inase  moreover and thereby preventing  the glycogen synthesis and increase  glucose levels  anyway there are complex hormonal  regulation now glycogen breakdown  happens via stepwise manner so in the  glycogen breakdown process  there would be a glucose generation in  muscle and liver which could be used for  a generation of  atp ultimately it would be broken down  into glucose 6-phosphate that broken  down glucose 6-phosphate can be  channeled into glycolysis pathway to  generate atp whenever it is necessary  and this process is known as  glycogenolysis in starved state or in  conditions of high energy demand this is  predominant process now glycogen  phosphorylase was would kind of like  chop off uh specific residues from the  sides of these glycogen chain  and whenever it encounters a branch  point then it is replaced with a  de-branching enzyme and the d branching  enzyme cleave of the branch point and  specially it leaves off that alpha 1 6  glycosidic linkage and then de-branching  enzyme disengage further glycogen  phosphorylase can chop off  linear branches  ultimately glucose 1-phosphate is  released due to this de-branching  procedure anyway phosphoglucomutase then  converts this glucose one phosphate in a  sequential fashion to glucose 6  phosphate now glucose 6 phosphate is now  broken down into glucose by the enzyme  called glucose 6 phosphatase  now let's talk about the classification  of glycogen storage disorder glycogen  storage disorder can be classified into  several groups like type 0 or lewis  disease  type 1 vongeric disease  type 1b  type 2 which is known as pompeo's  disease type 3 forbes and corey disease  type 4  anderson's disease type 5 type 6 and  many other types as well  now  beyond all these classifications and  everything what we should see and notice  that in all these cases the organs which  are highly affected are liver and muscle  and that's pretty much expected because  glycogen metabolism is happening in the  liver and my muscles predominantly  that's why these are the organs which  would which are expected to be affected  anyway this is the overall glycogen  synthesis pathway and we are going to  talk about few selected glycogen storage  disorders just to get us some just to  get some idea that what really happens  under these kind of diseases first we  are going to talk about the von garrick  disease which occurs due to a defect in  glucose 6 phosphatase enzyme  then we'll talk about mcardle and  hearth's disease which happens due to  the problem in glycogen phosphorylase  enzyme lastly we'll talk about corey's  disease which is a problem due to the  de-branching enzyme  now let's talk about the von gerick's  disease at the first place this is known  as type 1 glycogen storage disorder in  this case glucose 6-phosphatase enzyme  which converts glucose 6-phosphate into  glucose is actually  not functional due to some kind of  mutations which is inherited in this  case the baby  shows symptom as early as the age of  three to four so in during infancy the  symptoms are pretty visible and the  organs that are affected are liver  kidney and muscles  sometimes intestines are also  affected  affected infants very often show very  low blood sugar levels and sometimes  that lead to seizures so overall these  kind of babies show enlargement of the  liver known as hepatomegaly enlargement  of the kidney known as  nephromegaly elevated levels of lactate  and uric acid in the blood  it can lead to uracimia and also it  shows elevated level of lipids and  triglycerides in the body  now let's talk about the cori's disease  which is another type of  glycogen storage disorder known as gsd3  now in cory's disease  it is kind of like an inherited disorder  where there is a buildup of abnormal  glycogen in the body  the accumulated glycogen is structurally  abnormal and overall it impairs the  function of the liver and muscles and  liver and the muscles are the obvious  organs which are affected symptoms  include low blood sugar excess amount of  fat in the blood elevated blood levels  of liver enzymes  sometimes muscle weakness and hypertone  hypotonia  now cory's disease is  caused due to a problem in the deep  branching enzyme deep branching enzyme  chops off the branch point of glycogens  so when there is a alpha one six  glycosidic linkage it is taken care of  by the d de-branching enzyme  in absence of de-branching enzyme there  is a faulty branching in the glycogen  which leads to abnormal glycogen  formation  so the first sign of these kind of  disease is basically very poor muscle  tone or hypotonia eventually there could  be problems in cardiac muscle as well so  there could be cardiomyopathy  now glycogen phosphorylase enzyme  generally chops off all the linear  branches of the glycogen and releases  glucose one phosphate  so in mcardle disease and hearth's  disease this particular enzyme glycogen  phosphorylase is affected  so in macaroni disease muscle is  affected the most and in  hers disease the liver or the  hepatocytes are affected  in hers disease the science and symptom  begins from the infancy to early  childhood so there is hepatomegaly there  is increase in ketone bodies and  low blood sugar and there is also  increase in fat levels  there is also a slower growth rate mild  delay in motor coordination  the baby learn several motor skills like  dancing sitting walking etc in a lower  pace so there is overall problems  now in this video what we have learned  if we just summarize that we learned  glycogen storage disorder occurs due to  the problem in glycogen metabolism and  glycogen breakdown especially  and several enzymes involved in this  pathway can be affected and they are  classified into different different  subcategories and they have different  symptoms though these symptoms varies  from one to another but there is a  overarching similarity between all of  these symptoms that is hepatomegaly and  there is myopathy that means the muscle